WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:34.899
Hi! I am Tom Cech. And I've been asked to talk about my favorite experiment.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.132
Why did I pick  this particular experiment?

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:41.199
Well... It was one that I did with my own hands,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.099
and there is something special about discovering something through your own work in the lab.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.966
That is, as much as I enjoy working with students and postdoctoral fellows

00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:53.232
and exalt over their discoveries,

00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:57.999
something special about being able to make a discovery by yourself.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.866
This came when we were looking at initially the transcription

00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:07.066
of a large ribosomal RNA precursor

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.732
in a single cell pond animal, Tetrahymena thermophila.

00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:15.166
The gene that we were looking at made an RNA that

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:19.066
was interrupted by a stretch of non-coding sequence,

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:26.066
called an intron. And at this time, RNA splicing had been discovered a few years earlier,

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:32.032
but people knew very little about the mechanism by which an intron containing RNA

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:37.866
would be spliced to rejoin these two flanking sequences called exons

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:44.299
and release that intron RNA.  Now we had found in our studies of transcription of this gene

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:54.266
that the RNA splicing was taking place outside of the cell, in vitro, with our purified extracted RNA.

00:01:54.267 --> 00:02:03.166
So this seemed to be an opportunity for us to investigate the mechanism of RNA splicing.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:09.466
Initially this splicing reaction was taking place in the same cocktail

00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:13.899
of small molecules that were necessary for transcription.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:20.266
So I decided that it would be useful to see which of those small molecules

00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:24.399
was really required, not for the transcription part, but for the RNA splicing.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.966
So I added them, I subtracted them one at a time from the transcription cocktail,

00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:35.199
I added them back.  And I found that there were only two small molecules that were required:

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.032
one was a simple salt, magnesium chloride,

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:44.199
and the other was one of the precursors of RNA itself,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.366
guanosine triphosphate.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:51.766
A lot of specificity here: G was required, A wouldn't do it,

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:56.266
C wouldn't do it, U wouldn't do it. It had to be guanosine.

00:02:56.267 --> 00:03:01.566
And this seemed unusual, it seemed interesting,

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:06.366
but we didn't really know what to make of it

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.899
until someone else in the laboratory, Art Zaug,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.966
was sequencing the end of the intron

00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:16.432
after it was excised from the larger RNA.

00:03:16.433 --> 00:03:21.099
And he found that the sequence of the cut out RNA

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:27.832
started with a guanosine residue which is one of the four nucleotides of RNA.

00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:31.999
We didn't think much of it until we found out from Joe Gall's laboratory

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.466
at Yale university, that they had sequenced the gene that encodes this RNA,

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:44.166
and that they were adamant that there was no guanine

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:47.866
present near that exon-intron boundary.

00:03:47.867 --> 00:03:53.632
The rest of our sequences agreed between the two laboratories,

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:57.099
and so here we had a situation where it looked like maybe

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.766
there was a guanine that wasn't encoded by the DNA

00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:04.432
that was added to the end of the intron.

00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:07.299
And at the same time we had this evidence

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:13.232
from my earlier work that G was a required ingredient in the splicing reaction.

00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:18.666
So maybe these two observation were related to each other,

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:23.932
although the idea that one could just add a guanosine triphosphate

00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:28.732
to purified RNA and expect any chemical reaction,

00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:34.666
any cutting and joining reaction, to take place seemed unprecedented

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:38.632
and really quite farfetched. So, at the time

00:04:38.633 --> 00:04:42.466
I did this reaction rather, this experiment, rather quietly.

00:04:42.467 --> 00:04:46.666
I didn't tell any of the graduate students what I was doing.

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:49.299
I didn't want to look foolish if this reaction

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.332
failed as it was probably destined to do.

00:04:53.333 --> 00:05:01.632
When I added in one tube radiolabeled GTP to the purified precursor RNA

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:04.899
and in other tubes the other nucleotides

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.332
finally in the midst of my teaching schedule I had an opportunity to run the gel

00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:12.266
to look at the splicing products, and lo and behold,

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:17.099
only in the lane where I had added the radiolabeled GTP

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:23.966
was there a radiolabeled RNA band the exact size of the intervening sequence.

00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:31.232
So, I ran back to my office to try to get a little bit of peace and quiet

00:05:31.233 --> 00:05:36.266
to try to draw out what must be happening in terms of the splicing mechanism.

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:44.566
and what I quickly came up with was that this guanine that was added during the splicing reaction

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:48.799
was being joined to the 5' end of the intron.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.799
So it looked like it was attacking the splice site phosphate,

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:58.532
and forming a new oxygen-phosphorus bond that hadn't been there before.

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:04.899
Now if that happened, that would explain how the GTP would be covalently bound to

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:10.232
the end of the intron. But what would happen with the other product of this reaction?

00:06:10.233 --> 00:06:18.132
Well, the 5' exon would then have to be released with a hydroxyl group at its 3' end.

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:23.499
And exactly the same kind of chemical step if it occurred now

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.732
between this exon and the downstream splice site

00:06:26.733 --> 00:06:33.799
would result in ligation of the exons and release of the intron RNA

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:39.932
with this diagnostic guanosine at its 5' end.

00:06:39.933 --> 00:06:45.866
And, so, I thought, well,  has anyone... is this even chemically reasonable?

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:50.132
So, fortunately I had an organic chemistry textbook within reach,

00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:53.732
pulled it out. It did not discuss this sort of reaction

00:06:53.733 --> 00:07:03.099
with phosphate esters, but with esters of carbon, this was simply a trans-esterification reaction.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.032
So, what was happening...so there was precedent for this sort of reaction.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:10.599
You start out with one ester linkage,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:15.432
in this case a phospho-diester linkage, and you are now using

00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:19.899
the hydroxyl group of the ribosugar of guanosine

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.466
as a nucleophile attack at this site.

00:07:24.467 --> 00:07:31.532
It is also called an Sn2 reaction as you probably studied in your organic chemistry class.

00:07:31.533 --> 00:07:36.399
And this swapping of partners in this ester linkage

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.132
turned out to be the key mechanism of RNA splicing.

00:07:41.133 --> 00:07:45.099
So although it was exciting to have this mechanistic information

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:51.766
about RNA splicing, the question of the catalyst that was allowing all of this to happen eluded us

00:07:51.767 --> 00:07:56.599
for another year. We were assuming that there had to be a protein enzyme

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:02.232
that was responsible for a reaction that took place with this incredible specificity,

00:08:02.233 --> 00:08:08.532
after all in this long RNA there was only this one site that was being chosen as a splice site.

00:08:08.533 --> 00:08:11.066
There was also specificity with respect to guanosine

00:08:11.067 --> 00:08:16.666
relative to the other nucleotides. And the reaction was speeded up

00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:20.266
many billions of fold faster than

00:08:20.267 --> 00:08:22.732
a spontaneous phospho-transesterification reaction

00:08:22.733 --> 00:08:25.466
would be predicted to occur.

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:31.166
So, if all biological catalysts are protein enzymes, where was the protein?

00:08:31.167 --> 00:08:37.099
And we spent a lot of time looking for protein contaminants in our pure RNA preparation.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:44.299
Finally, out of a lack of being able to identify any, we switched around the hypothesis

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:51.066
and said, maybe it is just the RNA that is folding up to form the catalytic center for this reaction.

00:08:51.067 --> 00:08:55.832
To test that idea we were able to make an artificial transcript

00:08:55.833 --> 00:08:59.032
that had never seen the inside of a Tetrahymena cell

00:08:59.033 --> 00:09:03.699
and when we added guanosine triphosphate to that artificial RNA

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:08.132
the addition of G to the end of the intron

00:09:08.133 --> 00:09:13.399
and the RNA splicing that ensued convinced us that it was time to announce

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:19.499
that RNA could be an enzyme, that RNA had catalytic activity.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.766
This turned out to be the experiment, or the set of experiments,

00:09:23.767 --> 00:09:28.799
that resulted in the Nobel Prize in Chemistry 8 years later,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.432
but I think it is important to understand that at the time,

00:09:32.433 --> 00:09:35.632
we were not driven by the possibility of getting awards

00:09:35.633 --> 00:09:43.399
or recognition. It was simple curiosity about how does RNA splicing occur

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:48.899
and how could this reaction even be occurring with pure RNA

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.166
that was driving us in the laboratory

00:09:52.167 --> 01:00:00.000
and giving us so much satisfaction.

